<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rheinfels Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1245</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Burg Rheinfels</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rheinfels Castle is a ruined castle above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sankt-goar">Sankt Goar</a> on the left bank of the Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, built in 1245 by Count Dieter V of Katzenelnbogen to collect Rhine tolls. It withstood a siege by the Rhenish League in 1255–1256, became the residence of Landgrave Philip II of Hesse-Rheinfels and grew into one of the largest fortresses on the Rhine. French revolutionary troops blew it up in 1797.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rheinfels-castle.jpg" alt="Rheinfels Castle" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phantom3Pix · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burg_Rheinfels_Bild_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
